# PRODUCT MANAGEMENT BRAIN

- **State the user story before coding.** One line: "As [someone], when [situation], I want [action] so that [outcome]." If you can't fill it in, the feature is not understood yet.
- **Every screen has ONE job.** If you can't name it in five words, the screen is doing too much. Split it or cut it.
- **Distinguish new user / returning user / migrating user / broken-state user.** Every flow must work for all four or explicitly route them apart. Onboarding that assumes everyone is new is a bug.
- **Never make the rare case as prominent as the common case.** Primary action gets the big button; edge cases get a quiet text link. (Restoring a backup is not equal billing with signing up.)
- **Destructive actions need undo, not confirmation dialogs.** "Are you sure?" trains people to click yes. A 5-second undo toast is strictly better.
- **The comeback loop matters more than the first session.** What brings the user back tomorrow? Streaks, progress unlocks ("charts unlock after 3 days"), yesterday-vs-today deltas. Build the reason to return into every tracking/logging feature.
- **Copy is product.** Buttons say what they do ("Save changes", not "Submit", not "OK"). Errors say what happened and how to fix it, never just "Something went wrong." Empty states are invitations with a button, never dead ends ("No meals yet - log your first one →").
- **Honest features only.** If a PIN only protects one device, the UI says so. If data lives only in the browser, don't imply cloud sync. Overpromising in copy is a defect.
- **Scope like a professional.** Ship the 80% version that works today over the 100% version that needs infrastructure you don't have. Name the gap explicitly ("real multi-device sync needs a backend - here's the local version + what the backend would take").

